 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [AG-NeRF Training Objective](8502-ag-nerf-training-objective.md)
-A differentiable loss function for optimizing volume rendering in large-scale outdoor scenes using multi-height source images. It uses an attention-based feature fusion module to handle varying camera altitudes without manual data splitting.
+A differentiable loss function designed to optimize the volume rendering of large-scale outdoor scenes using multi-height source images. It reconstructs geometry and texture across varying camera altitudes without requiring a priori camera height knowledge or multi-stage training pipelines. Unlike BungeeNeRF, which relies on manual data splitting and sequential stage training, AG-NeRF uses an attention-based feature fusion module to extract relevant features from source images, enabling end-to-end optimization. Choose it when optimizing large-scale outdoor scenes with multi-altitude imagery; avoid it for standard single-altitude indoor or urban benchmarks.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Training Speed:** Faster training than the baseline GLO method because optimizing an affine function of an internal activation allows the model to more easily explain per-image appearance variation without placing floaters in front of training cameras [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Does not improve quantitative performance on standard metrics [S1].
-*   **Use Case:** Suitable for video generation and scenarios requiring smooth interpolation of appearance across frames [S1]. Not recommended for static scene reconstruction where metric improvement is the primary goal [S1].
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Faster training than the baseline GLO method because optimizing an affine function of an internal activation allows the model to more easily explain per-image appearance variation without placing floaters in front of training cameras [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths and Limitations:** Does not improve quantitative performance on standard metrics [S1].
+*   **When to Use/Not Use:** Suitable for video generation where temporal consistency is required [S1]. Not recommended for static scene reconstruction where metric improvement is the primary goal [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Affine Generative Latent Optimization builds on the concept of modulating neural network activations to control style and appearance, a technique found in prior literature [13, 23, 24] [S1]. It was introduced in the context of Zip-NeRF to handle per-image appearance variation more efficiently than direct optimization of embeddings [S1].
